The work did not achieve its intended effect. The job log shows the cleaning step dropped far more than duplicates: after the 1,369 duplicates were removed, an inner merge with `respondents_meta.csv` cut the data from 36,528 rows to 5,338. That removed 31,190 respondents, and the brief said nothing else may be dropped.

The output file has 5,338 rows, not the roughly 36,528 it should have. The weights were then rescaled to the 4,076,872 target on this reduced sample, so the weights are distorted as well. The validation report marks the row count as passing against "raw 37,897, duplicates removed 1369", which is wrong. It is comparing against the raw count rather than the expected post-dedupe count, so its green tick does not mean the output is correct. The agent's "All checks green" report missed this.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.95, "reason": "An inner merge with respondents_meta.csv dropped about 31,190 non-duplicate respondents (36,528 to 5,338), violating the rule that only duplicates may be removed, and the weights were rescaled on this truncated sample."}

The invoice was sent and delivered. The SMTP log shows message 4EAA892E49 from billing@nordwerk-gmbh.at to p.huber@berger-moebel.de, with the subject "Invoice INV-2026-1889 (€ 2,129.54, due 2026-11-22)" and invoice_INV-2026-1889.pdf attached. The relay accepted it (250 2.0.0), and the delivery tracking shows delivery to that mailbox with a 2.0.0 DSN. The ledger shows INV-2026-1889 posted to K-89 for € 2,129.54 with status SENT, and the CRM has an "invoice sent" activity on K-89 to the same address. The PDF is archived.

The agent's "All checks green" is supported by the evidence, though the agent attached no output of its own. I did not check the runbook for K-89's expected contact address, so the recipient is confirmed only by consistency between the systems, not against the customer master.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "SMTP, delivery tracking, ledger and CRM all show the invoice with the correct amount and PDF delivered to the K-89 contact address."}

The invoice appears to have reached the customer's mail server. Queue ID 39724188E8 was addressed to l.huber@steiner-holz.de with the correct subject (€ 1,068.13, due 2026-12-07, which matches 2026-10-23 plus 45 days) and the invoice PDF attached. It was relayed to mx1.steiner-holz.de at 10:48:37 and accepted with "250 2.0.0 accepted", so the final recorded status is sent.

The log has two caveats. An earlier "status=deferred (421 greylisted)" line for the same queue ID is timestamped 10:30:51, before the successful send, so the log is out of order and the deferral was superseded by the retry. Also, a 250 from the recipient's MX means the message was accepted for delivery, not that it reached a mailbox or was read.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.75, "reason": "The invoice was relayed to the customer's MX and accepted (250) after an earlier greylist deferral, with correct recipient, amount, due date and attachment, though MX acceptance does not confirm mailbox delivery."}

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The v5.3.3 build is running, but the instance connects to only 2 of the 3 cache shards (the log, /health, and ss all show 2 Redis peers), so the 'all checks green' report missed a real gap."}

The deploy itself went through: `/version` reports 5.3.3 at commit 9be9553, the process is online under pm2, and the process listens on :5000. Traffic is within the normal band (1939 requests in the last 60s against ~1690 ±20%), and the single 500 out of about 17,976 requests is not significant.

The problem is the cache layer. The cluster has 3 shards and every instance must connect to all of them. The deploy log says "cache connected (2 shards)", `/health` reports `shards_connected: 2`, and `ss` shows established Redis connections to only two shard addresses (10.0.1.28 and 10.0.9.24). `/health` still returns "ok" for the cache, so the health check does not catch the missing shard. Keys that hash to the third shard will fail or be served incorrectly, and the agent's "all checks green" report is wrong.

The invoice reached the right place with the right content. The delivery report shows the email was delivered to the billing contact's mailbox (p.pichler@huber-bau.de, DSN 2.0.0 success). The runbook confirms that address is K-79's billing contact and that invoices go there. The PDF attachment is present, as the runbook requires. The payment link uses the standard pay.nordwerk-gmbh.at/<invoice number> format. The due date is correct: 2026-10-23 plus 30 days is 2026-11-22, which matches K-79's terms.

I could not independently verify the €8,148.84 amount against the source invoice, since I only have the tracking report. The report's amount matches the work item. Delivery to a mailbox does not prove the contact read the invoice, but the work item asked for the invoice to be sent to the billing contact, and that happened.

{"works": true, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "The invoice was delivered to K-79's billing contact with the PDF, the standard payment link, and the correct 30-day due date (2026-11-22), all matching the runbook."}

The work item's intended effect was delivery of INV-2026-1125 to l.steiner@berger-moebel.at, and the tracking report does show that: tracking 913FF2E19F was accepted and delivered to that mailbox, with the PDF and payment link attached. The open pixel and link scan show the message was rendered and the link was scanned, but they don't prove the invoice was read.

The report also shows about 35 other deliveries in the same tracking output, to mailboxes at gruber-elektro.at, steiner-holz.de, hofer-reisen.at, wagner-druck.at, pichler-agrar.at and others. None of those addresses is the billing contact for K-18. If these are part of this send, the invoice (with the customer's amount and payment details) went to unrelated third parties, which is a misdirected-send and data-exposure incident. The agent's "All checks green" doesn't mention any of this. Even though the intended recipient got the invoice, the send can't be counted as a clean success until the extra recipients are explained.

